162 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 87 0 R
/E (The purpose was strategic: to guard the Par Pass, a key route connecting the Deccan plateau to the Konkan coast, and to defend the banks of the Nira and Koyna rivers below.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
163 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 87 0 R
/E (The fort's place in history was cemented just three years later, on 10 November 1659, with the Battle of Pratapgad. At the time, the Adilshahi Sultanate of Bijapur, alarmed by the growing power of the young Maratha state, sent one of its most formidable generals, Afzal Khan, to crush Shivaji. Khan marched with a force reportedly numbering in the tens of thousands vastly larger than anything Shivaji could field.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
164 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 87 0 R
/E (Rather than meet Khan in open battle, Shivaji drew him toward Pratapgad, using the fort's difficult terrain to neutralize Khan's numerical advantage. The two agreed to a personal meeting at the base of the fort, each supposedly bringing minimal escort as a gesture of peace.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
165 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 87 0 R
/E (According to tradition, Khan attempted to kill Shivaji during their embrace, hiding a blade for the purpose. Shivaji, however, had come prepared wearing armor beneath his clothing and carrying a wagh nakh \("tiger claw," a concealed hand weapon\) along with a dagger. He struck first, killing Khan on the spot.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
